{"cuisine/berber-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/berber-cuisine","n":"Berber cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["The Berber cuisine or Amazigh cuisine, though lacking a singular and standardized culinary framework, encompasses a diverse range of traditional dishes and influenced by the numerous flavours from distinct regions across North Africa. There is no consistent Berber cuisine, and it has been exposed to various influences. The Imazighen's meal choices were shaped by local availability of foods and personal finances. The Imazighen follow the same dietary laws and hygiene requirements as other Muslims. Ken Albala noted that \"Describing meals as typically Berber (Amazigh) is impossible–at best, they are samples of what is eaten in different regions by Berber (Amazigh) families\"."],["Amazigh cuisine differs from one area to another within North Africa and West Africa (Mauritania). For this reason, every dish has a distinct identity and taste according to its specific region of origin in North Africa, with some dishes estimated to be more than a thousand years old. Zayanes of the region of Khénifra around the Middle Atlas have a simple cuisine. It is based primarily on corn, barley, ewe's milk, goat cheese, butter, honey, meat, and game."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"berber-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berber_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Couscous","dish/couscous"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f7/Moroccan_cuisine-Couscous_lamb_berber.jpg/500px-Moroccan_cuisine-Couscous_lamb_berber.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"TheGirlsNY","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moroccan_cuisine-Couscous_lamb_berber.jpg"}},"cuisine/korean-regional-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/korean-regional-cuisine","n":"Korean regional cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in South Korea","intro":[["Korean regional cuisines are characterized by local specialties and distinctive styles within Korean cuisine. The divisions reflected historical boundaries of the provinces where these food and culinary traditions were preserved until modern times."],["Although Korea has been divided into two nation-states since 1948 (North Korea and South Korea), it was once divided into eight provinces according to the administrative districts of the Joseon period. The northern region consisted of the Hamgyong, Pyongan, and Hwanghae Provinces. The central region consisted of the Gyeonggi, Chungcheong, and Kangwon provinces. The Gyeongsang and Jeolla Provinces made up the southern region."],["Until the late 19th century transportation networks were not well developed, and each provincial region preserved its own characteristic tastes and cooking methods. Geographic differences are also reflected by the local specialty foodstuffs depending on the climate and types of agriculture, as well as the natural foods available. With the modern development of transportation, and the introduction of foreign foods, Korean regional cuisines have tended to overlap and integrate. However, there are still many unique traditional dishes in Korean regional cuisine that have been handed down through the generations."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"korean-regional-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_regional_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"dish/auberge-restaurant":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/auberge-restaurant","n":"Auberge (restaurant)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Auberge was a restaurant in Amsterdam, Netherlands. It was a fine dining restaurant that was awarded one Michelin star in 1980 and 1981."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-auberge-restaurant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auberge_(restaurant)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","ref":"site/pardonyourfrench-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","ref":"site/lacuisinedegeraldine-fr","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","ref":"site/newcaledonia-travel","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["'t Fornuis","dish/t-fornuis"],["'t Misverstant","dish/t-misverstant"],["'t Vlasbloemeken","dish/t-vlasbloemeken"],["&moshik","dish/moshik"],["Aan de Poel","dish/aan-de-poel"],["Adrian (restaurant)","dish/adrian-restaurant"],["Adventureland Bazaar","dish/adventureland-bazaar"],["Alain Ducasse at The Dorchester","dish/alain-ducasse-at-the-dorchester"],["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Alex (restaurant)","dish/alex-restaurant"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Arpège (restaurant)","dish/arpege-restaurant"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Atelier Crenn","dish/atelier-crenn"],["Au Coin des Bons Enfants","dish/au-coin-des-bons-enfants"],["Au Crocodile","dish/au-crocodile"],["Au Pied de Cochon (Mexico City)","dish/au-pied-de-cochon-mexico-city"],["Auberge De Kieviet","dish/auberge-de-kieviet"],["Aubergine (London restaurant)","dish/aubergine-london-restaurant"],["Auguste (restaurant)","dish/auguste-restaurant"],["Bagatelle (restaurant)","dish/bagatelle-restaurant"],["Balthazar (restaurant)","dish/balthazar-restaurant"],["Bardot Brasserie","dish/bardot-brasserie"],["Bâtard (restaurant)","dish/batard-restaurant"],["Baumé (restaurant)","dish/baume-restaurant"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Be Our Guest Restaurant","dish/be-our-guest-restaurant"],["Beaulieu, Doorwerth Castle","dish/beaulieu-doorwerth-castle"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Bell's (restaurant)","dish/bell-s-restaurant"],["Belmond Le Manoir aux Quat'Saisons","dish/belmond-le-manoir-aux-quat-saisons"],["Beluga (restaurant)","dish/beluga-restaurant"],["Berowra Waters Inn","dish/berowra-waters-inn"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Bistro 990","dish/bistro-990"],["Bistro Agnes","dish/bistro-agnes"],["Bistro Jeanty","dish/bistro-jeanty"],["Bistro Moulin","dish/bistro-moulin"]]}]},"ingredient/pig-fat":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pig-fat","n":"Pig fat","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Pig fat may refer to:"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pig-fat"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pig_fat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Chorizo","ingredient/chorizo"],["Lacón","ingredient/lacon"],["Phaseolus vulgaris","ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rapini","ingredient/rapini"],["White bean","ingredient/white-bean"]]}]},"dish/cendol":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cendol","n":"Cendol","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Cendol, also known as lot chong, mont let saung (Burmese: မုန့်လက်ဆောင်း), nom lut (Khmer: បង្អែមលត), lod song (Lao: ລອດຊ່ອງ) and bánh lọt, is a traditional Southeast Asian dessert characterised by soft, green, worm-like jelly strands made from rice flour or mung bean starch, coconut milk and palm sugar syrup, typically served over shaved ice. The jelly is flavoured with pandan and combined with a creamy coconut milk base and the caramel-like sweetness of palm sugar. It is widely consumed in Brunei, Cambodia, Indonesia, Malaysia, Laos, Myanmar, Singapore, Thailand and  Vietnam, and forms an established component of Southeast Asian food culture, commonly sold by vendors at roadsides, hawker centres and food courts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cendol"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cendol"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Ice","ingredient/ice"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Shaved ice","ingredient/shaved-ice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","ref":"site/dailycookingquest-com","colo